## Supported deployment mode

This browser script supports a **clean first provision** and resuming the
**same generated release** after a partial failure. It is not evidence of a
general in-place schema upgrade. Before writing, an existing list must carry
this declaration's exact provenance marker and match its immutable template.
Existing fields must match internal name/type/writability and lookup target.
Only then are declared mutable settings narrowly reconciled and read back,
including required, unique/indexed, default, Choice members and type-specific
display/storage properties. Unknown ownership or unsupported shape aborts for
explicit migration.

> **Fail closed:** if a declared list/library already exists from another
> release or its provenance is unknown, do not run this script. Keep the site
> inactive and use an approved versioned migration/rebuild procedure. Never use
> generated rollback against a site containing real records.

## How to run this deployment

1. **Assess the site first**: paste `assess.js.txt` (read-only; it makes no
   changes) into the target site's browser console and read
   `assess-manifest.md`. The final `[SP-ASSESS] [DONE]` verdict must be
   **COMPATIBLE**, or a **DEGRADED** whose findings you have reviewed and
   accepted, whether they warn or report that nobody could check the
   requirement. Resolve **BLOCKED** before going further. `deploy.js.txt`
   runs this same assessment itself as its first phase and enforces the
   verdict: **BLOCKED** aborts and cannot be overridden; **DEGRADED**
   aborts unless `const ACKNOWLEDGE_DEGRADED = true;` is set near the top
   of the script and it is pasted again.
2. **Review this manifest**: the Summary below must show 0 validation
   errors, and the lists/ACLs must be what you intend to provision.
3. **Open a CLASSIC page of the target site**, signed in as a Site Owner:
   `{{ site_url }}/_layouts/15/settings.aspx`
   (modern pages may not expose `_spPageContextInfo`, which the script's
   wrong-site safety check requires; it aborts anywhere but the target site).
4. **Open the browser console** (F12 -> Console). If the browser blocks the
   paste, type `allow pasting` and press Enter first.
5. **Paste the entire contents of `deploy.js.txt`** and press Enter. (If a run
   feels slow, flip `const DEBUG = false` to `true` near the top of the
   pasted script before running: every REST call logs its timing and a
   per-phase seconds table prints before DONE. No rebuild needed.)
6. **Watch the `[SP-DEPLOY]` log lines.** The script runs read-only preflights
   (permissions, list ownership and existing-schema shapes), then Phase {{ phase_num['security'] }} permission levels and
   groups, then lists -> columns -> lookups -> indexes -> ACLs -> seeds, and prints
   a summary object: `listsCreated`, `columnsCreated`, `columnsSkipped`,
   `errors`. Success is `errors: []`.
7. **On any `[ERROR]`**, stop and read it; the script fails closed rather
   than guessing. Fix the stated cause, then paste the SAME script again:
   reruns are safe for this release (completed work is verified and skipped).
   Only `[SP-DEPLOY] [ERROR]` lines and a non-empty `errors` array are
   failures. The browser may paint stray read-probe requests red; those
   are absence checks the script handles.
8. **Verify the clock cells**, when the bundle carries `verify.js.txt`:
   paste it into the same console, on the same site. It writes only to one
   hidden scratch list (`_dbml-verify`) and prints a `[SP-VERIFY] [DONE]`
   verdict; expect **VERIFIED**. A **MISMATCH** means a date rule, view
   window or default this pack relies on does not behave on this site as
   measured: read its FAIL lines before trusting the deployed rules.
9. **Verify**: run the pack's verification checklist (its deploy.md /
   runbook) before handing the site over.
10. **`rollback.js.txt` is ONLY for abandoning a failed FIRST provision on an
   empty site**, never against a site containing real records (see the
   fail-closed note above).

